{
  "id": 219475525,
  "iid": 10438,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "Fix dissection of DNS Service Discovery SRV answers",
  "description": "The DNS dissector was handling names in SRV answers by splitting the name\ninto labels, and assigning the first as the \"service\", the second as the\n\"protocol\", and the rest as the \"name\". This works for normal SRV records\n([RFC 2782](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc2782)) such as `_http._tcp.example.com`.\n\nHowever, the DNS-SD specification ([RFC 6763](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6763)) which is often used with\nmulticast DNS ([RFC 6762](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6762)) adds the concept of \"instance names\", where the\nname in SRV records could be `InstanceName._http._tcp.local`. This was\nbeing incorrectly dissected as `InstanceName` service, `_http` protocol,\nand `_tcp.local` domain name.\n\nThis commit fixes the dissection by checking if the third label starts with\nan underscore, in which case we assume it's the protocol (like `_tcp`) and\nuse the first label as instance name (new dissector field).\n\nWhile this is a heuristic, I believe it will work for any correct usage of the protocol. Non-spec-compliant record names (eg. `_tcp.example.com` or `multiple.labels.here._http._tcp.local`) may get split unexpectedly, but they shouldn't cause crashes.\n\nFix #8483",
  "state": "merged",
